---
import Eyebrow from "./Eyebrow.astro";
import Screenshot from "./Screenshot.astro";
import Card from "./Card.astro";
import agents from "../assets/agents.png";
---

<section
  id="agents"
  class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-page flex-col gap-stack px-gutter pt-section"
>
  <Eyebrow>Agents in the loop</Eyebrow>

  <h2 class="max-w-[900px] font-display text-h2">
    Your agents take part in the review &mdash; they aren&#39;t just the subject
    of it.
  </h2>

  <p class="max-w-[730px] text-body text-muted">
    Install GitWarren into your agent as a plugin. It brings more than the
    connection: a note that teaches the agent the habit &mdash; open a review
    when it has finished changing code, hand you the link, then read your
    comments before it starts the next thing. You stop having to ask.
  </p>

  {/*
    The install block. The Homebrew line elsewhere on the page is one command
    and fits on a centred line; this is four, across three families of tool, so
    it gets a box instead — the Card's own border and surface, so a new shape
    is not introduced for it. `select-all` on each line for the same reason as
    the Homebrew one: one click takes the whole command.

    Left-aligned and `overflow-x-auto`, because a command must not be centred
    into ambiguity or wrapped into a second line that looks like a second
    command.
  */}
  <div
    class="flex flex-col gap-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-[11px] border border-line bg-surface p-card max-w-[730px]"
  >
    <p class="font-mono text-note text-faint">
      <span class="text-muted">Claude Code</span><br />
      <code class="text-accent select-all"
        >/plugin marketplace add klarluft/gitwarren-app</code
      ><br />
      <code class="text-accent select-all">/plugin install gitwarren@gitwarren</code>
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-note text-faint">
      <span class="text-muted">Gemini CLI</span><br />
      <code class="text-accent select-all"
        >gemini extensions install https://github.com/klarluft/gitwarren-app</code
      >
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-note text-faint">
      <span class="text-muted">Any agent, the note on its own</span><br />
      <code class="text-accent select-all">npx skills add klarluft/gitwarren-app</code>
    </p>
    <p class="text-cardbody text-faint">
      Codex, Cursor, VS Code and Kiro read the same repository from their own
      plugin screens.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="max-w-[730px] text-body text-muted">
    Underneath it is an MCP server over stdio, and through it the agent gets the
    same seventeen tools the app itself uses: open a review, read the whole
    discussion, reply in a thread, leave a comment on a line, resolve one. Any
    MCP client can be pointed at it by hand instead. Ask an agent to explain its
    own diff, or to answer the question you left on line 40 &mdash; in the
    review, where the answer stays.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src={agents}
    alt="An agent review comment and a human reply in the same thread"
    caption="An agent’s comment and the reply, in one thread — attributed, and never mistakable for a person."
    class="mt-[28px] max-w-shot max-sm:hidden"
  />

  <div class="mt-[38px] grid grid-cols-1 gap-cards lg:grid-cols-3">
    <Card title="Always attributed">
      A machine-written comment is always marked as one, and the tool&#39;s name
      comes from the MCP handshake rather than from whatever the model decides to
      call itself that day.
    </Card>
    <Card title="Two agents stay two">
      Each MCP session is given its own id, so two agents working at the same
      time are told apart in the thread without either having to cooperate.
    </Card>
    <Card title="Yours to edit">
      You can edit or delete anything in a review. An agent is held to its own
      messages &mdash; it can fix its own typo, not quietly rewrite yours.
    </Card>
  </div>
</section>
